**New evidence**
- UNICEF Ethiopia Annual Report 2022, UNICEF, 2023-04
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Annual Report 2022","publisher":"UNICEF Ethiopia","publication_date":"2023-04","url":"https://www.unicef.org/ethiopia/reports/annual-report-2022","claim":"UNICEF said children and families in Ethiopia faced multiple emergencies in 2022 and that UNICEF continued investing in long-term development programming despite the challenge of balancing humanitarian and development needs.","locator":"Highlights section, lines 56-57","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct contemporaneous annual report with explicit target-year summary and clear institutional authorship.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly country-level context and UNICEF response under the Abiy Ahmed government; not a direct ruler-choice finding by itself.","contrary_evidence":["The page does not isolate Abiy Ahmed's personal decisions.","It emphasizes emergency need rather than successful welfare delivery."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.10"]}
- UNICEF Ethiopia press release, severe drought and humanitarian need, 2022-02-01
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Prolonged drought pushing families in Ethiopia to the brink","publisher":"UNICEF Ethiopia","publication_date":"2022-02-01","url":"https://www.unicef.org/ethiopia/press-releases/prolonged-drought-pushing-families-ethiopia-brink","claim":"UNICEF said drought and water shortages were pushing children and families to the brink, with more than 6.8 million people expected to need urgent humanitarian assistance by mid-March 2022, and that UNICEF was responding with borehole rehabilitation, emergency water trucking, malnutrition treatment, and emergency education and child protection support.","locator":"lines 57-66","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Ethiopia-specific press release with detailed figures and response description.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but relevant to the Abiy Ahmed government's crisis-management environment; implementation was through UNICEF with local authorities.","contrary_evidence":["The source is primarily a humanitarian assessment, not proof of ruler competence.","It describes a severe inherited shock environment."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8"]}
- UNICEF Ethiopia photo essay, drought and climate change, 2022-01-05
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Drought and climate change pushing communities to the brink","publisher":"UNICEF Ethiopia","publication_date":"2022-01-05","url":"https://www.unicef.org/ethiopia/stories/drought-and-climate-change-pushing-communities-brink","claim":"UNICEF described drought-driven water scarcity, rising malnutrition, school disruption, and emergency water trucking in Borena, while working with zonal and woreda administrations to respond.","locator":"lines 59-60 and 73-78","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct Ethiopia field report with concrete crisis effects and response measures.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly crisis-context evidence with local-authority response; only indirect attribution to the Abiy Ahmed government.","contrary_evidence":["The piece shows deprivation and disruption more than successful state protection.","It is a localized field story rather than a national policy assessment."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8"]}
- UNICEF Ethiopia nutrition programme launch, 2022-07-01
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Government of Ethiopia launches a $30 million nutrition programme to reduce maternal malnutrition, child wasting and stunting","publisher":"UNICEF Ethiopia","publication_date":"2022-07-01","url":"https://www.unicef.org/ethiopia/press-releases/government-ethiopia-launches-30-million-nutrition-programme-reduce-maternal","claim":"The Government of Ethiopia launched a multisectoral nutrition programme, implemented with UNICEF and Action Against Hunger, to reach at least one million pregnant women and three million children over five years with health and nutrition services across 10 regions.","locator":"lines 55-63 and 65-76","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct joint press release with explicit program design, scope, and target groups.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and about a current government-led intervention.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Abiy Ahmed government through the Ministry of Health; implementation was shared with UNICEF, donors, and NGOs.","contrary_evidence":["The source also says Ethiopia still had very high stunting and wasting burdens.","Funding and implementation were partnership-based rather than purely national."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- UNICEF Ethiopia drought response campaign, 2022-12-22
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"A campaign to save lives","publisher":"UNICEF Ethiopia","publication_date":"2022-12-22","url":"https://www.unicef.org/ethiopia/stories/campaign-save-lives","claim":"UNICEF said a lifesaving 'Find and Treat' campaign was underway in Borena in collaboration with the Government in Ethiopia, and that more than 133,600 children aged 6-59 months had been screened in two rounds for severe acute malnutrition treatment.","locator":"lines 59-61 and 104-106","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct Ethiopia-specific response story with named government collaboration and concrete delivery metrics.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Abiy Ahmed government's drought-response environment through collaboration with government health authorities.","contrary_evidence":["The story reflects a rescue response to severe deprivation, not a solved welfare problem.","UNICEF funding also mattered materially."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.9"]}
- WHO Ethiopia joint government-UN drought mission, 2022-02-22
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"A joint Government of Ethiopia and United Nations Ethiopia high-level mission visits drought affected communities in Somali and Oromia regions","publisher":"WHO Regional Office for Africa","publication_date":"2022-02-22","url":"https://www.afro.who.int/fr/node/16152","claim":"A high-level mission led by Ethiopia's health and agriculture ministries, with WHO, FAO and UNICEF support, visited drought-affected communities, delivered medicines and nutrition supplies, and supported water-quality testing and stabilization centers for severe acute malnutrition.","locator":"news release text describing the mission, supply deliveries, and drought-response activities","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct official mission report with ministerial participation and concrete service-delivery actions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Abiy Ahmed government through line ministries; the operational response was joint with UN agencies.","contrary_evidence":["This is a response to a severe drought emergency, not evidence that the crisis was prevented.","The source does not isolate national-level performance from donor assistance."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.6"]}
- WHO Ethiopia emergency medical teams, 2022-11-25
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ethiopian Emergency Medical Teams' first deployment provides support to drought-affected areas","publisher":"WHO Regional Office for Africa","publication_date":"2022-11-25","url":"https://www.afro.who.int/countries/ethiopia/news/ethiopian-emergency-medical-teams-first-deployment-provides-support-drought-affected-areas","claim":"WHO reported that a 17-member Ethiopian emergency medical team was deployed to Gode in June 2022, screened 211 children, and admitted about 100 children with severe acute malnutrition to treatment.","locator":"lines 212-215","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct operational report with concrete caseload and implementation details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 deployment and delivery.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Ethiopian health authorities under the Abiy Ahmed government, with WHO training and coordination.","contrary_evidence":["It is a localized emergency response and not proof of systemwide service quality.","It reflects the severity of the underlying drought crisis."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.6","6B.9"]}
- WFP Ethiopia hunger update, 2022-06-23
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Conflict, climate and soaring food prices push Ethiopia further into hunger while WFP funding runs out","publisher":"World Food Programme","publication_date":"2022-06-23","url":"https://www.wfp.org/news/conflict-climate-and-soaring-food-prices-push-ethiopia-further-hunger-while-wfp-funding-runs","claim":"WFP said more than 20 million people in Ethiopia were facing hunger, that 19 months of war had left more than 13 million in need of food assistance in the north, and that humanitarian supplies were flowing after the government's humanitarian truce.","locator":"lines 262-267","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct contemporaneous WFP emergency reporting with explicit statistics and a government-truce linkage.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed partly to the Abiy Ahmed government's truce-enabled access policy, but mostly reflects inherited conflict and climate shocks.","contrary_evidence":["The source also shows the scale of deprivation and aid dependence.","Aid flow was not uniform and fuel constraints persisted."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.10"]}
- WFP refugees in Ethiopia, 2022-08-09
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"WFP, UNHCR, RRS appeal for funding to continue feeding over 750,000 refugees in Ethiopia","publisher":"World Food Programme","publication_date":"2022-08-09","url":"https://www.wfp.org/news/wfp-unhcr-rrs-appeal-funding-continue-feeding-over-750000-refugees-ethiopia","claim":"WFP, UNHCR and Ethiopia's Refugees and Returnees Service appealed for funds to keep feeding over 750,000 refugees after ration cuts and warned that vulnerable families faced undernutrition and increased protection risks.","locator":"lines 255-261","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct joint appeal with explicit numbers and Ethiopia government participation through RRS.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Abiy Ahmed government only indirectly through the state refugee service; the main driver was funding shortfall and displacement pressure.","contrary_evidence":["This is more a sign of unmet need than successful welfare provision.","The source shows donor dependence and ration cuts."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.7","6B.8","6B.10"]}
- World Bank Ethiopia statement, 2022-12-01
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement on World Bank Engagement in Ethiopia","publisher":"World Bank Group","publication_date":"2022-12-01","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/statement/2022/12/01/world-bank-group-statement-on-current-situation-in-ethiopia","claim":"The World Bank said it was supporting Ethiopia's demands for basic human services including education, food security, health, clean water, livelihood support, women's empowerment, and social and environmental protection, and noted past gains in enrollment, child mortality, and access to clean water.","locator":"lines 24-27","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct World Bank statement with clear country-specific service domains and quantified past gains.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly in 2022, though the gains described are longer-run.","ruler_attribution":"Primarily country-context and partnership evidence under the Abiy Ahmed government; not a direct individual decision record.","contrary_evidence":["The statement is institutional and does not isolate Abiy Ahmed's own choices.","It acknowledges conflict and climate shocks as major pressures."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- World Bank urban safety net feature, 2022-03-10
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Empowering Ethiopian Women for Climate Resilience through the Urban Productive Safety Net","publisher":"World Bank Group","publication_date":"2022-03-10","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/feature/2022/03/10/empowering-ethiopian-women-for-climate-resilience-through-the-urban-productive-safety-net","claim":"The World Bank said Ethiopia was implementing productive safety-net programs for vulnerable households, especially women, and that the Urban Productive Safety Net Project had benefited 604,000 urban poor people in 11 cities and was expanding to 84 cities with cash transfers, basic social services, and public works.","locator":"story highlights and lines 148-150, 282-283","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct World Bank feature with concrete beneficiary counts, service design, and expansion details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and about an ongoing government-linked program.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Abiy Ahmed government through the social-protection apparatus, with heavy World Bank partnership and local delivery.","contrary_evidence":["The program is donor- and partnership-supported, not purely national.","It addresses vulnerability in a context of major macro and climate stress."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.7","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- UNICEF Ethiopia Bete/My Home education response, 2022-05-17
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Welcome to 'Bete' - My Home","publisher":"UNICEF Ethiopia","publication_date":"2022-05-17","url":"https://www.unicef.org/ethiopia/stories/welcome-bete-my-home","claim":"UNICEF said the 'Bete' programme was designed to provide a safe space for boys and girls by integrating accelerated learning, child protection and skills development, and that it had reached close to 194,000 children in seven regions, including around 5,000 children in Afar.","locator":"lines 59-61","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct Ethiopia-specific education response story with concrete reach figures and a clear design goal.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Abiy Ahmed government's education/emergency environment through UNICEF and local partners, but not to Abiy personally alone.","contrary_evidence":["This is a remedial humanitarian education program rather than proof of broad national educational success.","It depends on partners and external support."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.9"]}
- UNICEF/European Union education restoration and school feeding, 2022-12-09
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"EU donates €33 million (1.8 billion ETB) to UNICEF and WFP to restore essential education services and implement school feeding programmes in conflict-affected areas in Ethiopia","publisher":"UNICEF Ethiopia","publication_date":"2022-12-09","url":"https://www.unicef.org/ethiopia/press-releases/eu-donates-33-million-18-billion-etb-unicef-and-wfp-restore-essential-education","claim":"UNICEF said EU funding would restore education services and implement school feeding in conflict-affected areas, noting that nearly two million children were out of school due to conflict and that over 8,500 schools in conflict-affected regions had been damaged or destroyed.","locator":"lines 54-60","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct Ethiopia-specific joint funding announcement with explicit scale of educational disruption and restoration plan.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Abiy Ahmed government's conflict-affected education environment; implementation was partner-led.","contrary_evidence":["The source is mainly about repair and recovery from conflict damage.","The funding is external rather than a demonstration of domestic fiscal capacity."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}
- WHO Ethiopia health financing assessment, 2022-06-08
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Health Financing Progress Matrix assessment: Ethiopia 2022","publisher":"World Health Organization","publication_date":"2022-06-08","url":"https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240067554","claim":"WHO said the assessment identified strengths and weaknesses in Ethiopia's health-financing system and set out priority directions for future reforms, with the latest UHC and health expenditure indicators presented for policy use.","locator":"overview section, lines 225-225 on the open page","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct WHO publication with clear policy relevance, though it is a system assessment rather than a ruler-choice record.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly institutional/system context under the Abiy Ahmed government rather than a direct individual act.","contrary_evidence":["The report is diagnostic rather than proof of reform delivery.","It implies substantial remaining weaknesses in the health-financing system."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.9"]}
- World Bank Ethiopia education PEIR, 2022-05-31
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ethiopia - Education Sector Public Expenditure and Institutional Review","publisher":"World Bank Group","publication_date":"2022-05-31","url":"https://documents.worldbank.org/en/publication/documents-reports/documentdetail/099615206172211408","claim":"The World Bank said access and availability of education had increased significantly in Ethiopia over the last decade, but COVID-19 and recent political unrest increased unfinished learning, reduced participation, and amplified inequalities.","locator":"search-result excerpt and report metadata","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank country report, but the browser access was limited and the best accessible wording comes from the search result summary.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly around 2022 and explicitly discussing the recent period.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly country-system context under the Abiy Ahmed government, with limited direct attribution to his choices.","contrary_evidence":["The claim mixes long-run progress with shocks and is not a clean ruler-performance measure.","It says unrest and the pandemic worsened learning and participation."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.5","6B.8","6B.10"]}
- UNICEF Ethiopia humanitarian education response, 2022-12-09
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"EU donates €33 million (1.8 billion ETB) to UNICEF and WFP to restore essential education services and implement school feeding programmes in conflict-affected areas in Ethiopia","publisher":"UNICEF Ethiopia","publication_date":"2022-12-09","url":"https://www.unicef.org/ethiopia/press-releases/eu-donates-33-million-18-billion-etb-unicef-and-wfp-restore-essential-education","claim":"The education restoration plan included UNICEF's 'My Home-Bete' approach, which combines accelerated learning, child protection, and life skills training for conflict-affected children.","locator":"lines 59-60","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct program description in a dated Ethiopia-specific press release.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Abiy Ahmed government's conflict-disruption environment and recovery effort, with implementation by UNICEF and WFP.","contrary_evidence":["This is a post-damage recovery program, not evidence of avoided harm.","It is externally funded and partner-delivered."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.9"]}
- UNICEF Ethiopia safe-space learning response, 2022-05-17
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Welcome to 'Bete' - My Home","publisher":"UNICEF Ethiopia","publication_date":"2022-05-17","url":"https://www.unicef.org/ethiopia/stories/welcome-bete-my-home","claim":"UNICEF said the Bete initiative was an education program providing safe space linked with child protection services for displaced and emergency-affected children.","locator":"lines 59-61","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct Ethiopia-specific program story with a clear service model and target population.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly attributable to the Abiy Ahmed government through the emergency education environment and local implementation partnerships.","contrary_evidence":["The program is localized and remedial rather than a systemwide reform proof.","It depends on external support and local partnerships."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.8","6B.9"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I reviewed roughly 30 documents across UNICEF, WHO, WFP, World Bank, FAO, ILO, UNESCO, and related Ethiopia pages. I kept 13 distinct URLs as the strongest non-duplicative evidence and rejected the rest as redundant, too generic, out-of-period, or blocked.

I removed several broad global education and learning-crisis reports because they were useful context but not Ethiopia-specific enough for ruler attribution. I also screened out 2024-2026 retrospective pages unless they directly described a 2022 program or 2022/23 condition; most were too retrospective for the target year. A few potentially useful items were blocked or not fully accessible in-browser, including some IMF and ILO pages, so I did not rely on them where the accessible text was too thin. I also avoided repeating the same drought, nutrition, and education facts through multiple publishers when one strong source already established the claim.

The retained picture is mixed. There is real evidence of welfare-oriented crisis response and program design in 2022: nutrition, drought relief, emergency health teams, school restoration, and safety-net expansion. But the same year also shows very severe humanitarian need, widespread drought and conflict disruption, major education loss, refugee ration cuts, and heavy dependence on donors and partners.

**Remaining gaps**
- Direct evidence that Abiy Ahmed personally drove or supervised these social-welfare actions is still limited; most evidence is government, ministry, or partner implementation rather than his own decision record.
- Better target-year household survey data disaggregated by region, gender, displacement status, and poverty would sharpen distribution and dignity judgments.
- Stronger audit-style evidence on whether welfare and social protection were allocated fairly, or used selectively, would help.
- More direct budget-execution or expenditure-tracking evidence for health and education in 2022 would improve attribution and durability assessment.
